/**
@mainpage risersim -- corotational-beam FEM for riser and mooring analysis

@section overview Overview

`risersim` is an open-source C++ reimplementation of the static and dynamic
analysis engine behind ANFLEX (the closed-source riser/mooring finite-element
solver at `trunk/src`). It models a riser or mooring line as a chain of 3D
corotational beam elements (risersim::CorotationalBeam3D), solved with a
full-step Newton-Raphson nonlinear iteration inside a direct linear solver
(risersim::LinearSolver).

The design deliberately mirrors ANFLEX's class layout where the physics
matters (element formulation, friction model, artificial-stiffness
stabilization, linear-solver symmetry/reordering), while modernizing the
C++ around it (RAII, `std::unique_ptr` ownership, an abstract
risersim::Integrator / risersim::LinearSolver plug-in layer where ANFLEX has
`cIntegrator` / `cLinearSolver`). Every place the implementation follows a
specific ANFLEX source file is called out in a comment, so the two can be
cross-checked line by line.

@section layers Architecture layers

| Layer            | Classes                                                                 | ANFLEX equivalent                       |
|-------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|
| Linear solver     | risersim::LinearSolver, risersim::EigenSimplicialLDLTSolver, risersim::EigenSparseLUSolver | `cLinearSolver` / `cLinearSOE`           |
| Equation reordering | risersim::compute_rcm_order()                                          | `cuthill_mckee_reorderer.cpp`            |
| Integrator        | risersim::Integrator, risersim::StaticIntegrator                        | `cIntegrator` / `cStaticIntegrator`      |
| Analysis (nonlinear loop) | risersim::Analysis, risersim::StaticAnalysis, risersim::DynamicAnalysis | `cAnflexAnalysis`, `cNewtonRaphson`      |
| Element           | risersim::CorotationalBeam3D                                            | `cBeam` (`beam.cpp`)                     |
| Node              | risersim::Node3D                                                        | `cNode`                                  |
| Model             | risersim::RiserModel                                                    | `cDomain`                                |
| Environment       | risersim::SeabedInteraction, risersim::CurrentProfile, risersim::MorisonForce, risersim::JONSWAPSpectrum | `soil.cpp`, current/wave loading modules |
| Prescribed top motion | risersim::VesselOffset                                              | `cLoad` / `cTimeSeries`                  |

@section fidelity_notes Key fidelity findings (see docs/mapa_classes_anflex_estatica.md)

A companion document, `docs/mapa_classes_anflex_estatica.md` (Portuguese,
development log), records the investigation that produced several of the
non-obvious design decisions baked into this code:

- risersim::CorotationalBeam3D keeps a **fixed reference triad** per node,
  set once at t=0 (`node1_init_triad`/`node2_init_triad`), and measures local
  deformational rotation against it every iteration -- mirroring ANFLEX's
  `calc_init_rot_mt`. Feeding the *total accumulated* rotation into the
  bending stiffness instead (the original risersim bug) collapses
  convergence far earlier than ANFLEX for long chains.
- risersim::rodrigues() / risersim::compose_rotations() compose finite 3D
  rotations properly (matrix/quaternion composition), because naive vector
  addition is wrong for rotations that don't commute -- mirroring ANFLEX's
  `pseudo_sum`.
- risersim::SeabedInteraction::calculate_friction_1d() is an
  **incremental elastic-plastic** Coulomb friction model driven by the
  per-iteration displacement increment plus persistent force state
  (risersim::Node3D::friction_force), not by total accumulated displacement
  -- mirroring `soil_uncoupled.cpp::calc_unidimensional_friction`.
  risersim::Analysis::assemble_system() decomposes that increment into local
  axial/lateral directions (not global X/Y) before calling it twice, also
  mirroring `soil.cpp::calc_transf_matrix`.
- risersim::compute_rcm_order() and risersim::EigenSimplicialLDLTSolver
  close a solver-fidelity gap: ANFLEX defaults to a symmetric direct solver
  (Skyline) with Reverse Cuthill-McKee bandwidth reduction applied at
  model-build time, regardless of which of its three backends
  (Skyline/MKL/Pardiso) is selected -- neither iterative nor preconditioned.
  Both changes were validated as pure, behavior-preserving refactors (same
  reference results across the regression battery).
- ANFLEX itself has **no line search / step-cutting / damping** in its
  Newton-Raphson loop (confirmed by reading `newton_raphson.cpp` and
  `integrator.cpp::update()`) -- risersim's optional backtracking line
  search (in `static_analysis.cpp`) is an extension beyond ANFLEX, added as
  a safety net against contact-induced divergence, not a fidelity match.

@section open_problems Known open problems

Documented in detail in `docs/mapa_classes_anflex_estatica.md`, not yet
resolved:
- Long isolated chains (roughly 300-350 elements) hit a convergence
  threshold tied to the artificial-stiffness exponential decay rate.
- Combined seabed + current loading diverges catastrophically in longer
  chains (33+ elements near touchdown; the full 500-element model).
- A 20-element seabed-only case chatters (tiny oscillating residual) right
  at the elastic/plastic friction yield boundary, introduced by the
  axial/lateral friction decomposition.

@section building Generating this documentation

    cd risersim
    doxygen Doxyfile

Output lands in `docs/doxygen/html/index.html`. If CMake found Doxygen at
configure time, `cmake --build build --target docs` does the same.
*/
